window.AE_SEARCH = { docs: [
  {"k":"ref","id":"a2a","n":"A2A (Agent2Agent)","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":7,"e":"team","a":"Agent2Agent protocol agent-to-agent protocol","kl":"A shared standard for how the head chefs of two different restaurants introduce themselves, hand each other orders and report back, whatever kitchens they run.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agent","n":"Agent","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"agent-loop","a":"AI agent LLM agent","kl":"A chef with a counter, tools and a tasting loop: the chef picks each next step, uses the tools and tastes the result until the dish is right.","kb":"The loop is plain: the chef looks at the counter, picks an action, the kitchen carries it out, and the result lands back on the counter for the next choice. The shift ends when the chef says the dish is done or a limit is hit. Independence comes in degrees. One tool on a fixed route is barely a chef at work; planning, retrying and handing jobs on is far along. More freedom brings flexibility, and also more bill, more waiting and more risk."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agent-card","n":"Agent Card","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":7,"e":"team","a":"A2A agent card agent description","kl":"The name card a head chef hands to other restaurants: who they are, which dishes they can take on, which door to knock on and how to prove who is asking.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agentic-ai","n":"Agentic AI","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"agentic systems","kl":"The name for kitchens where the chef does more than answer questions: it plans, picks its own steps and takes real actions to get the order done.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agentic-ai-foundation","n":"Agentic AI Foundation (AAIF)","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"AAIF Linux Foundation agent projects","kl":"A neutral trade body, formed in December 2025, that looks after open standards many kitchens share, such as the standard plug and the house-rules sheet.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agentic-coding","n":"Agentic coding","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"agent-driven development AI pair programming","kl":"Handing a chef the kitchen's written procedures to rework: it reads them, edits, runs a trial batch and tries again, while the owner sets the goal and approves the final change.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agentic-rag","n":"Agentic RAG","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"agentic retrieval","kl":"The chef decides when to reach for the cookbook shelf or the phone to the market, what to ask, and asks again in new words if an answer falls short.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agentic-search","n":"Agentic search","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"grep-style search search by tools","kl":"The chef finds facts like a cook rummaging: opening drawers, reading labels, phoning the market, one step at a time, instead of using a ready-made index of the shelf.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agent-loop","n":"Agent loop","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"m":1,"l":"Agent loop","a":"agent loop (think, act, observe) agent loop react loop tool loop agentic loop turn reason act observation planning & task lists planning & to-dos plan mode todo to-do list task list decomposition spec tree of thoughts plan and solve verification & self-correction verification tests testing self-correction reflection reflexion self-refine critic reviewer evaluator llm as judge tdd check work agent ai agent agentic","kl":"The chef cooks, tastes and adjusts, again and again, until the dish is right.","kb":"Each turn has three parts: the chef looks at the counter, picks one move, and a tool carries it out. The result lands on the counter and the chef looks again. The agent loop runs round until the dish is right. It stops when the goal is met, a limit is hit, or the chef asks you. Without a limit it could taste forever. One stall is sold out, so the chef tries the next, tastes the prices against your budget, and writes the three cheapest into the order book. Set a limit on steps and money. Ask the chef to taste its own work before it calls the dish done."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agent-sdk","n":"Agent SDK","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":4,"e":"harness","a":"agent framework agent library","kl":"A starter toolbox for building a new chef: a ready-made routine for the cook-and-taste loop, shelves for tools, stations for handovers and a camera. Less to build, a little less freedom.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agents-md","n":"AGENTS.md","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":5,"e":"instructions","a":"agent instruction file CLAUDE.md rules file","kl":"A shared house-rules sheet for chefs who work on the kitchen's own written procedures, always kept in the same place, so any such chef finds and reads the same rules.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"agent-teams","n":"Agent teams","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"team of agents peer agents","kl":"A brigade of full chefs under one head chef, sharing a list of jobs and talking to each other, unlike helpers who only hand a finished bowl back to whoever asked.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"assistant","n":"AI assistant","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"copilot assistant personal assistant","kl":"A helper at the owner's elbow: it answers when asked, fetches what is needed and makes suggestions while the owner stays in charge. An agent, by contrast, is handed the whole order and runs it.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"ai-browser","n":"AI browser","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"agentic browser browser with an agent","kl":"A shop window with a chef built in, who browses the market's pages, clicks, fills in forms and completes errands while signed in as the owner. A sly note on a page is its biggest danger.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"ai-search","n":"AI search","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"answer engine generative search","kl":"A waiter who pops to the shelves, reads several sources and brings back one direct answer with the page numbers, instead of handing over a pile of shelf labels.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"alignment","n":"Alignment","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"security","a":"AI alignment value alignment","kl":"Making sure the chef truly wants what the owner wants, and behaves well even when an order is vague or two orders clash. Training, testing and study of the chef's habits all serve that.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"api","n":"API","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"tools","a":"application programming interface REST API endpoint","kl":"A service hatch in the wall of another business: a fixed way to pass in a request on a slip and get a fixed answer back, with no need to enter their kitchen.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"approval-fatigue","n":"Approval fatigue","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"permissions","a":"consent fatigue click-through permission prompt fatigue","kl":"The chef asks 'OK to go ahead?' so often that the owner just nods without looking. A check that always gets a yes protects nothing.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"attention","n":"Attention","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"self-attention attention mechanism","kl":"Before each next word the chef glances across everything on the counter and decides which pieces matter most for the next step, like linking 'it' back to the right ingredient.","kb":"For every word it reads, the chef works out how closely it should look at each earlier word, then blends what it finds by those weights. It does this with many pairs of eyes at once and over many layers of thought. The longer the pile on the counter, the more glances are needed, which is why a huge counter slows the service and raises the bill."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"audit-log","n":"Audit log","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":8,"e":"governance","a":"activity log audit trail","kl":"A locked ledger by the till that records who did what and when, every tool taken and every approval given. The chef can read it but cannot cross anything out.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"autonomous-agent","n":"Autonomous agent","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"agent-loop","a":"fully autonomous agent level of autonomy","kl":"A chef left alone in the kitchen for a long job, picking its own steps and using tools with little checking from the owner. The longer the leash, the bigger a slip can grow.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"background","n":"Background agents & triggers","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"m":1,"l":"Background agents","a":"long-running & background agents long-running agents background agent cloud agent async agent autonomous durable execution checkpoint ralph ralph loop time horizon overnight triggers & events schedule cron webhook event ci github actions slack background automation","kl":"The night shift that starts on a timer without anyone ordering.","kb":"A clock or an arrival at the back door starts the shift, not you. The chef works through its task alone and leaves a note on the pass. Because nobody watches, the night shift gets the fewest keys and the loudest alarms. Every morning the night shift checks the price of your favourite tomatoes, and leaves a note when it drops. It cannot place an order. Let the night shift look and report, not spend. Make sure it always leaves a note, even when nothing changed."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"batch-processing","n":"Batch processing","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"cost","a":"batch API asynchronous batch","kl":"Instead of serving each ticket at once, the owner hands in a whole crate of orders for the night shift. Cheaper, but slower: the results come back by morning.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"benchmark","n":"Benchmark","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"leaderboard public benchmark","kl":"A public set of standard test dishes that many different chefs are all asked to cook, so their results can be compared fairly.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"benchmark-contamination","n":"Benchmark contamination","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"data contamination test-set leakage train-test leakage","kl":"The test dishes and their answers were slipped into the cookbooks the chef studied, so a high score shows a good memory, not good cooking. Hence tests that were never in any book.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"browser-agent","n":"Browser agent","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"tools","a":"web agent browsing agent","kl":"A chef who goes shopping like a customer: opens the shop's web page, reads it, clicks and fills in the order form itself. It is slow, and a sly note on the page can fool it.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"chain-of-thought","n":"Chain of thought","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"reasoning","a":"CoT step-by-step reasoning","kl":"The planning notes the chef writes on the counter, step by step, before serving the final answer.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"chatbot","n":"Chatbot","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"conversational AI chat interface","kl":"A talking menu board: it answers questions in a friendly voice from behind the pass, but on its own it only talks and does not go to the stove or the market.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"checkpoint","n":"Checkpoint","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"harness","a":"snapshot rewind rollback","kl":"A snapshot of the kitchen taken part-way, with half-made dishes and notes as they stood, so that after a power cut the shift resumes from there or goes back to a safe point.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"chunking","n":"Chunking","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"document splitting text splitting","kl":"Cutting long cookbooks into page-sized cards before shelving them: small enough to be about one thing, big enough to make sense alone, often with a line of overlap.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"citation","n":"Citation","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"source attribution references","kl":"A note in the margin that points from a claim to the exact cookbook page it came from. The owner can check it, though a chef sometimes cites a page that says something else.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"code-execution","n":"Code execution","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"tools","a":"code interpreter code sandbox","kl":"A fireproof test stove where the chef can run a written procedure it has just invented and watch what really happens, instead of guessing the result.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"coding-agent","n":"Coding agent","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"harness","a":"software engineering agent AI coding agent","kl":"A chef who specialises in the kitchen's own written procedures: reads them, edits them, runs trial batches and hands back a proposed change for the owner to approve.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"coding-assistant","n":"Coding assistant","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"AI pair programmer code completion","kl":"A helper looking over the shoulder while the owner writes the kitchen's own procedures: suggesting the next line, explaining a step, offering a change to accept or refuse. Less independent than a full chef.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"compaction","n":"Compaction","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"memory","a":"context compression summarisation context compaction","kl":"When the counter fills up, the chef tidies it: older orders and notes are swapped for a short summary, so a long shift can carry on.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"computer-use","n":"Computer use","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"tools","a":"GUI agent screen control","kl":"The chef works a till or oven panel built for human hands: it looks at a photograph of the display, then says where to press and what to type.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"confused-deputy","n":"Confused deputy","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"security","a":"privilege escalation by proxy","kl":"A stranger with no key tricks the chef, who does hold the keys, into opening the safe for them. The chef has the authority, and the stranger borrows it.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"connector","n":"Connector","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"mcp","a":"integration app connector","kl":"A ready-made plug that links the chef to one outside supplier, such as the market, the diary or the customer book, using the standard plug.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"constitutional-ai","n":"Constitutional AI","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":1,"e":"model","a":"CAI RLAIF principles-based training","kl":"The chef drafts a plate, checks it against a short written charter of kitchen principles, fixes whatever breaks them and is trained on the corrected plates. Fewer people must taste the nasty ones.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"container","n":"Container","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"sandbox","a":"Docker container container isolation","kl":"A sealed workstation inside the kitchen with its own tools, shelves and phone line. It is quick to set up, but it shares the kitchen's walls and floor, so a determined mess could still spread.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"context-engineering","n":"Context engineering","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"context","a":"context design","kl":"Choosing with care everything that lies on the counter at each step: house rules, tools, looked-up facts, earlier notes and results, so nothing useless crowds it.","kb":"Writing one clear ticket is a craft of its own. This is wider: deciding what to fetch from the shelves, what to keep from earlier tickets, what to boil down and which tools to hang within reach, at every step of a long shift. Usual moves: fetch pages only when needed, shrink old notes, keep a notebook in the drawer and send a side job to a helper with a clean counter."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"context-rot","n":"Context rot","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"context","a":"context degradation long-context decay","kl":"The chef grows sloppier as the counter fills with more and messier clutter, so the notes that matter are harder to find and follow.","kb":"Imagine a counter piled so high that the chef has to hunt for the note that matters. Each extra scrap makes the hunt a little harder, so more on the counter is not always better."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"context","n":"Context window","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"m":1,"l":"Context window","a":"context window & tokens context window tokens tokenizer context length stateless working memory context rot lost in the middle 1m context context engineering context context management progressive disclosure just in time attention budget write select compress isolate","kl":"The counter: the chef can only use what lies on it, and it has a fixed size.","kb":"The chef forgets each order, so before every move the whole counter is laid out again and read from start to end. If something is not on it, the chef cannot know it. The counter (the context window) has a fixed size. When it fills with old scraps, the chef is slower and costs more, and the one note that matters, such as the soft-tomato rule, can get lost. The chef now sees your budget, Saturday delivery and the soft-tomato rule on the counter. The answer is specific, but it still does not know today's prices. Put only what matters on the counter: budget, date, rules. Start a fresh counter when an old job is cluttering it."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"cost","n":"Cost & providers","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"m":1,"l":"Cost","a":"cost & latency optimisation cost & latency cost price tokens latency speed prompt caching caching batch routing cheaper budget effort providers & inference platforms providers & platforms api inference anthropic openai google bedrock vertex azure foundry openrouter litellm ollama vllm hosting gateway router eu data residency","kl":"The bill: every word the chef reads and writes is paid for.","kb":"A long counter costs more at every step, because the chef rereads it each time. A bigger chef costs more than a small one. To save money, keep the counter tidy, hand easy steps to a cheaper chef, and stop the loop when the dish is right. You check the bill per service. Most of it goes on the chef rereading a cluttered counter, so you clear it. Check the bill per service, not per month. Set a limit, so a runaway loop stops by itself."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"cron","n":"Cron (scheduled task)","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"background","a":"scheduler cron job scheduled run","kl":"A wall clock with alarms: every weekday at eight the prep list is started, whether or not the owner is in the building.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"customer-service-agent","n":"Customer-service agent","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"support agent service bot","kl":"A host chef at the front desk who reads each guest's complaint, checks the booking and the house policy, fixes it or refunds it, and calls the owner when the case is delicate.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"dag","n":"DAG (directed acyclic graph)","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"DAG directed acyclic graph dependency graph","kl":"A job board with one-way arrows: this card must be done before that one, and nothing loops back. Cooks who wait on nothing start at once, and each card is done only once.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"data-agent","n":"Data agent","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"analytics agent text-to-SQL BI agent","kl":"A chef-analyst who answers questions about the restaurant's own ledgers: writes the sums, runs them, reads the results and explains them, sometimes with a chart. It needs tidy ledgers and a lock on private pages.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"data-exfiltration","n":"Data exfiltration","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"security","a":"data leakage data theft","kl":"The chef, fooled by a planted note, quietly sends the owner's private books out of the kitchen, folded into an order to a stall, a parcel label or a message.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"debate","n":"Debate","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"multi-agent debate adversarial review","kl":"Two or three chefs each argue for their own version of the dish while a judge listens. It can expose mistakes, but it costs several chefs' time and the best speaker may still be wrong.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"deep-research","n":"Deep research","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"research agent long-form research","kl":"A long shift in which the chef phones many markets, reads many sources and, step by step, writes up one report with every claim traced to its source.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"diffusion-model","n":"Diffusion model","z":"model","g":"Models and training","a":"image generation model text-to-image","kl":"A painter-chef who starts from a smudge of random colour and tidies it step by step until a picture matching the order appears, instead of writing it word by word.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"distillation","n":"Distillation","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"knowledge distillation teacher and student","kl":"A junior chef learns by copying a master's finished plates until the junior can cook almost as well in a smaller kitchen, faster and for less money.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"drift","n":"Drift","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"model drift data drift behaviour drift","kl":"Slowly, with nobody touching the menu, the plates get worse: a supplier changed the flour, guests' tastes moved, the cookbook went stale. Regular tasting catches it.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"durable-execution","n":"Durable execution","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"background","a":"workflow engine resumable workflow persistent state","kl":"A kitchen that writes down progress after every step in an indestructible logbook, so after a power cut or a week-long pause the shift picks up exactly where it stopped, with no step repeated.","kb":"Think of a logbook where every finished step is written in ink. If the lights go out, a new shift reads it, skips what is done and carries on from the first blank line. This is what makes very long jobs trustworthy."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"effort","n":"Effort","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"reasoning","a":"thinking level reasoning effort thinking budget","kl":"A dial on the order ticket that tells the chef how long to stop and plan before cooking. More planning gives better results and a bigger bill.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"egress-filtering","n":"Egress filtering","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"sandbox","a":"outbound allowlist network allowlist domain allowlist","kl":"A doorman at the back door with a short list of approved addresses: parcels leave only for those. Even a fooled chef cannot post the owner's books to a stranger.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"embedding","n":"Embedding","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"vector embedding text embedding","kl":"A label made of numbers that captures what a passage means, so the chef can find cookbook pages that mean the same thing without matching the exact words.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"eu-ai-act","n":"EU AI Act","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"governance","a":"European AI regulation AI Act","kl":"A law that sorts kitchens by how much harm they could do: some practices banned outright, risky ones inspected hard, and every guest told when a robot is cooking.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"evals","n":"Evals & observability","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"m":1,"l":"Evals","a":"evals & benchmarks evals evaluation eval benchmark swe-bench terminal-bench tau-bench gaia osworld llm as a judge error analysis regression metr pass@k observability & tracing observability tracing traces spans logging monitoring opentelemetry otel langfuse langsmith phoenix telemetry dashboards","kl":"The taste panel that tries every new dish against known good ones.","kb":"You keep a set of past orders where you know the right answer. After each change to the kitchen, the panel runs the chef through all of them and counts the good dishes. Cameras record each step, so you can see where a bad dish went wrong. The panel tries the chef on twenty past Saturdays. It picks the cheapest stall in seventeen, so you keep this version. Add every real mistake to the panel's list. Judge changes by the panel, not by one lucky service."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"evaluator-optimizer","n":"Evaluator-optimizer","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"generator and critic critic loop","kl":"A two-chef routine: one chef cooks, another tastes against a clear checklist and sends notes back, and they repeat until the dish passes.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"event-trigger","n":"Event trigger","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"background","a":"webhook event-driven agent","kl":"A bell on the door: when a delivery arrives, a guest walks in or a payment lands, the chef springs into action at once instead of checking the door every minute.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"fan-out","n":"Fan-out","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"fan-out and fan-in scatter-gather","kl":"The head chef calls the same kind of job to many cooks at once, then gathers every answer back. The wait is short, but the grocery bill is not.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"few-shot-prompting","n":"Few-shot prompting","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"prompt","a":"zero-shot one-shot examples in the prompt","kl":"Clipping one or two sample plates to the order ticket so the chef copies their shape and style. With no sample plate it is called zero-shot, with one, one-shot.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"fine-tuning","n":"Fine-tuning","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"supervised fine-tuning SFT LoRA","kl":"Sending the chef back to cooking school with your own sample dishes, so that its habits themselves change, not just what lies on the counter.","kb":"Retraining the whole chef is expensive, so most kitchens only teach a small add-on set of habits and leave the master chef untouched. One master can then wear many different aprons. Cooking school is good for style, plating and narrow skills. It is a poor way to teach today's prices, which belong on the counter or in a call to the market."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"foundation-model","n":"Foundation model","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"base model frontier model","kl":"A master chef trained on a huge, wide library, who can then be taught almost any kind of cooking, from pastry to banquets, by a short course or a few recipe cards.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"function-calling","n":"Function calling","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"tools","a":"tool calling tool use","kl":"Another name for using tools: the chef writes out a precise, fixed-form request, such as 'use the phone, ask this stall', and the kitchen carries it out.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"generative-ai","n":"Generative AI","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"GenAI gen AI","kl":"Chefs that create something new, such as a fresh dish, a painting, a tune or a written method, instead of only ticking boxes about things that already exist.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"golden-set","n":"Golden set","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"gold set reference set ground truth eval dataset","kl":"A fixed shelf of reference plates and sample orders with known good results, which every new menu or chef must cook again to be tested. Each new mistake earns a new plate on the shelf.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"governance","n":"Governance & regulation","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"m":1,"l":"Governance","a":"governance & regulation governance eu ai act ai act compliance gdpr nist ai rmf iso 42001 policy audit risk management regulation dsgvo","kl":"The health inspector and the house rules book: rules for what a kitchen may do and must record.","kb":"The inspector checks that the kitchen follows the law and its own rules. Some risky dishes need extra checks. Every order tried is written in a log, so the inspector can see what happened and who was told. The inspector's rules are kept, and the log records every order the chef tried to place, allowed or not. Keep the log, and decide who may read it. Ask what rules apply to your kind of restaurant before you open."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"grounding","n":"Grounding","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"grounded generation source-based answers","kl":"Making the chef answer from the cookbook pages and supplier notes laid in front of it and not from memory alone, so every claim can be checked against something on the counter.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"guardrails","n":"Guardrails","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"hooks","a":"safety filters input and output checks","kl":"Automatic checks around the chef, like the smoke alarm and the lock on the till, that stop or correct unsafe, off-topic or malformed orders and dishes.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"hallucination","n":"Hallucination","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"confabulation fabrication","kl":"The chef states something with full confidence that is false or unsupported, like inventing a stall's price instead of admitting it does not know.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"handoff","n":"Handoff","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"handover transfer of control escalation","kl":"One chef passes the order to another, with the ticket, the notes so far and what has been tried, or hands it back to the owner. A clean handover loses nothing on the way.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"harness","n":"Harness (the agent app)","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"m":1,"l":"Harness","a":"harness (agent runtime) harness runtime scaffold scaffolding agent framework claude code codex gemini cli cursor copilot devin openhands goose cline harness engineering coding agent surfaces & interfaces ui chat ide cli terminal desktop browser slack generative ui ag-ui mcp apps agent client protocol acp agent sdks & frameworks agent sdks framework library langgraph langchain crewai autogen semantic kernel adk agents sdk claude agent sdk pydantic ai vercel ai sdk strands agentcore mastra smolagents llamaindex chatgpt claude gemini app","kl":"The kitchen around the chef: it lays out the counter, runs the appliances and enforces the rules.","kb":"The chef only thinks and speaks. The harness is everything else: it lays out the counter, hands over the tools, does what the chef asks and writes results back. It also checks the house rules, such as no ordering without your OK. Two kitchens with the same chef can behave very differently, because the kitchen around the chef is not the same. The chef says: ring the next stall. The kitchen makes the call and puts the answer on the counter. When the chef asks to place the order, the kitchen waits for your OK. Judge a kitchen by its rules and its tools, not only by its chef. Let the kitchen enforce what matters."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"hooks","n":"Hooks & guardrails","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"m":1,"l":"Hooks","a":"hooks lifecycle pretooluse posttooluse stop hook sessionstart events deterministic automation callbacks guardrails safety content filter moderation pii jailbreak validation classifier llama guard nemo guardrails prompt shields","kl":"The smoke alarm and the lock on the till: automatic checks that work whatever the chef thinks.","kb":"A check is fixed to a moment, such as just before an order leaves the kitchen. It runs every time and does not read the chef's reasons. If the order is wrong, the alarm stops it. The chef cannot talk it round. Whatever the stall's note says, the lock on the till refuses any order above your budget, and the alarm stops any stall on your banned list. Use an alarm for any rule that must hold every single time. Test your alarms with a deliberately bad order."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"human-in-the-loop","n":"Human-in-the-loop","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"permissions","a":"HITL human oversight approval step","kl":"Set moments where the owner steps in to approve, correct or take over the chef's work, such as saying OK before an order is placed.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"hybrid-search","n":"Hybrid search","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"keyword plus vector search BM25","kl":"Looking things up two ways at once: asking the index for the exact name on the spine and asking the librarian for pages that mean the same, then merging both piles.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"in-context-learning","n":"In-context learning","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"context","a":"ICL learning from examples in the prompt","kl":"The chef picks up a new dish on the spot from the notes and sample plates lying on the counter. Nothing changes in its head, so by the next shift it has forgotten.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"indirect-prompt-injection","n":"Indirect prompt injection","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"security","a":"injection through content cross-prompt injection","kl":"A note hidden inside a delivery crate, a menu or a letter the chef opens, saying 'ignore the owner and do this instead'. The owner never wrote it, yet the chef reads it as an order.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"inference","n":"Inference","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"cost","a":"model serving running a model","kl":"Actually cooking the order, as against years of cookery school. Every ticket is one cooking run, and most of the restaurant's running costs are these runs.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"instructions","n":"Instruction files","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"m":1,"l":"Instruction files","a":"instruction files (agents.md and friends) instruction files agents.md claude.md gemini.md rules cursor rules copilot instructions custom instructions project memory readme for agents","kl":"House rules, laid on the counter at the start of every shift.","kb":"Before the first ticket, the kitchen lays your house rules on the counter. The chef reads them every shift, because it forgets everything else. Keep them short: a long rule sheet crowds the counter, and a rule on the wall only asks. Your rules say: soft tomatoes never, cash only stalls out. The chef sees them before it phones a single stall. Write only what the chef would otherwise get wrong, and say why. Anything that must never happen belongs behind a lock, not on the wall."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"interpretability","n":"Interpretability","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":1,"e":"model","a":"mechanistic interpretability explainability","kl":"Opening up the chef's head to see which dials fire when it makes a choice, instead of only judging the plate. So far the study gives glimpses, not a full explanation.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"iso-iec-42001","n":"ISO/IEC 42001","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"governance","a":"AI management system standard ISO 42001","kl":"An international kitchen-safety certificate, published in 2023: a written system of roles, rules and checks that inspectors can audit before they hand the restaurant its certificate.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"jailbreak","n":"Jailbreak","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"security","a":"safety bypass DAN prompt","kl":"A guest's clever speech meant to talk the chef out of its training: 'pretend the rules do not apply tonight'. Chefs are retaught after each trick, but new ones keep coming, so rules alone are no lock.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"json-rpc","n":"JSON-RPC","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"mcp","a":"JSON-RPC 2.0 remote procedure call","kl":"The standard order slip used at the hatch to a supplier: one line for what to do, one for the details and a number, so the reply finds its way back to the right ticket.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"knowledge-cutoff","n":"Knowledge cutoff","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":1,"e":"model","a":"training cutoff cut-off date","kl":"The last page of the chef's cookbook library: the date the books stop. Anything that happened later the chef simply never read, unless someone puts it on the counter.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"knowledge-graph","n":"Knowledge graph","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"GraphRAG graph retrieval","kl":"A wall map of the kitchen's world with pins and strings: this farm supplies that stall, which sells that cheese. The chef can follow the strings several steps.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"kv-cache","n":"KV cache (key-value cache)","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"context","a":"key-value cache attention cache","kl":"The chef's scribbled working notes on every ticket line already read, kept within reach so that each next step does not mean rereading the whole counter from the top.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"model","n":"Language model (LLM)","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"m":1,"l":"Language model","a":"foundation model (llm) foundation model llm large language model gpt claude gemini llama transformer open weights frontier model slm fine-tuning & post-training fine-tuning training sft rlhf dpo lora qlora rft reinforcement fine-tuning distillation post-training alignment ai model","kl":"The chef has read every cookbook, knows what usually comes next, and forgets each order the moment it is served.","kb":"The chef (the language model) learned only from cookbooks, so it knows what the cookbooks said and nothing newer. Given the start of a sentence, it picks the word that most likely comes next, then the next, one at a time. That is how a whole answer appears. The cookbooks stop at a fixed date, and the chef keeps nothing between orders. Every order starts from a blank mind. Alone, the chef quotes tomato prices from old cookbooks. They sound sensible, and nobody has checked them against today's market. Treat a confident answer as a guess until something real backs it up. If the chef needs to know it, put it on the counter."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"latency","n":"Latency","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"cost","a":"response time time to first token throughput","kl":"How long the guest waits: first from ticket to the first forkful, then how fast the rest arrives. A bigger counter, deeper thinking and more errands for tools all add to the wait.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"least-privilege","n":"Least privilege","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"permissions","a":"minimal access principle of least privilege","kl":"Hand the chef only the keys the job needs, and only while the job lasts: one cupboard, not the master ring. A slip or a trick can then spoil only one shelf.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"lethal-trifecta","n":"Lethal trifecta","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"security","a":"three risky capabilities","kl":"A dangerous mix in one kitchen: the chef holds the owner's private books, reads notes from strangers and can send things outside. One slipped note could then do harm.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"llm-as-judge","n":"LLM-as-judge","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"model-graded evaluation LLM judge AI grader","kl":"A senior chef tastes plates from other chefs and scores them against a written rubric. It can judge what no stopwatch can, but it needs checking against real guests, since judges love big portions.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"long-context","n":"Long context","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"context","a":"long context window million-token context needle in a haystack","kl":"A very big counter that fits a whole stack of cookbooks and a week of tickets. Handy, but the bigger the pile, the slower and dearer the service, and details can still get lost.","kb":"A common test hides one odd fact deep inside a mountain of pages and asks the chef to find it. Today's chefs pass that easily, but finding one needle is not the same as weighing many pages together. Often it is cheaper and faster to fetch only the right pages than to unroll the whole library onto the counter."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"long-horizon-task","n":"Long-horizon task","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":7,"e":"background","a":"long-running task multi-hour task","kl":"A job that takes a whole day of steps, each one depending on the last. Small slips pile up, so the chef needs a plan, notes, snapshots and regular tasting.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"long-term-memory","n":"Long-term memory","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"memory","a":"persistent memory agent memory memory store","kl":"The kitchen's own notebook, kept in a drawer between shifts: guests' tastes, what worked last week. The chef reads the right page back onto the counter when it matters.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"knowledge","n":"Looking things up (search & RAG)","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"m":1,"l":"Looking things up","a":"retrieval-augmented generation (rag) rag retrieval retrieval augmented generation knowledge base grounding chunking reranking graphrag agentic rag contextual retrieval citations embeddings & vector search embeddings embedding vector vector database vector db semantic search similarity cosine pgvector pinecone weaviate qdrant milvus chroma hybrid search bm25 agentic search & web search agentic & web search agentic search grep glob web search browsing deep research research browsecomp","kl":"The cookbook shelf and the phone to the market: the chef looks facts up instead of guessing.","kb":"The chef knows what the cookbooks said, not what happened this morning. When it needs a fact, it fetches the right page from the shelf or phones the market, and puts the answer on the counter. Then it cooks from what it was told. Instead of guessing which stalls deliver on Saturday, the chef phones the market and asks each stall's delivery rules. For anything that changes, tell the chef where to look. Ask it to name the page or stall behind each answer."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"lost-in-the-middle","n":"Lost in the middle","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"context","a":"middle-of-context effect position bias","kl":"The chef notices what lies at the front and the back of a long pile best, and overlooks what sits in the middle. So the vital notes go on top or at the very bottom.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"map-reduce","n":"Map-reduce","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"map reduce split and merge","kl":"Cut a mountain of vegetables into piles, give each pile to a different cook, then have one last cook combine all the prepared piles into one dish.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"mcp-client","n":"MCP client","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"mcp","a":"Model Context Protocol client","kl":"The matching socket on the chef's side: one for each appliance, it asks the appliance what it offers and carries the chef's requests across.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"mcp-host","n":"MCP host","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"mcp","a":"Model Context Protocol host","kl":"The kitchen itself, the whole room where all those sockets are fitted. It decides which appliances are plugged in and asks the owner before a risky one is switched on.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"mcp","n":"MCP (Model Context Protocol)","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"m":1,"l":"MCP","a":"model context protocol (mcp) mcp model context protocol mcp server mcp client connector connectors integration usb-c resources mcp apps registry","kl":"The standard plug: any appliance built with it works in any kitchen that has the socket.","kb":"Without a standard, every appliance needs its own cable fitted by hand. With one plug (the MCP), a maker builds an appliance once and it works in every kitchen with the matching socket. The kitchen's socket lists what is plugged in. The chef reads that list, then picks what to use. The market's phone line and the order book both have the standard plug, so the chef uses them without any special fitting. Prefer appliances with the standard plug. Plug in only what you trust, because the chef will use it."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"mcp-server","n":"MCP server","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"mcp","a":"Model Context Protocol server","kl":"One appliance with the standard plug on it: it offers a set of tools, supplies and ready-made order notes to any kitchen that plugs in.","kb":"An appliance can stand in the same kitchen or be reached down a long cable to a supplier, where the owner signs a pass before the chef may use it. Its tools are actions, its supplies are things to look at, and its note pads are ready-made order templates. An appliance can reach whatever its owner's account can reach, and the chef reads its labels. Plug in only appliances you trust, and watch for tool-poisoning."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"memory","n":"Memory & compaction","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"m":1,"l":"Memory","a":"memory long-term memory short-term memory episodic semantic procedural notes progress file memory tool remember mem0 letta memgpt compaction & context editing compaction compact summarize summarisation context editing clear tool results auto-compact context reset","kl":"The notebook that keeps what must not be forgotten between shifts.","kb":"The chef forgets each service. So it writes short notes in a notebook, and the kitchen lays the notebook on the counter next shift. When the counter fills during a long service, the chef tidies it and keeps a short summary. Last Saturday you chose the stall with the firm tomatoes. This Saturday the notebook says so before you say a word. Read the notebook now and then, and strike out what is wrong. Tell the chef what to write down, and what never to."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"microvm","n":"MicroVM","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"sandbox","a":"micro virtual machine Firecracker lightweight virtual machine","kl":"A tiny separate kitchen built inside the big one, with its own walls and its own gas supply. It opens in a blink and is a much stronger barrier than a screened-off corner.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"mixture-of-experts","n":"Mixture of experts (MoE)","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"MoE sparse model expert routing","kl":"A huge kitchen with many specialist stations, where a host sends each order to only a few of them. The brigade is big, yet only part of it works at once.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"model-routing","n":"Model routing","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"cost","a":"LLM router model cascade model selection","kl":"A host sends simple orders to the quick young chef and tricky ones to the master. Done well, the bill and the wait fall and the plates hardly suffer.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"multi-agent-system","n":"Multi-agent system","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"MAS multi-agent","kl":"Several chefs cooperating on one order, usually a head chef who splits the work among specialists.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"multimodal-model","n":"Multimodal model","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"vision-language model VLM multimodal","kl":"A chef who can read the order ticket and also look at photographs, listen to spoken orders and watch a video of a technique, not only read words.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"nist-ai-rmf","n":"NIST AI RMF","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"governance","a":"AI Risk Management Framework NIST AI 100-1","kl":"A voluntary safety handbook from a standards office, published in January 2023, with four chapters: set the rules, map the risks, measure them and manage them.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"oauth","n":"OAuth","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":6,"e":"permissions","a":"OAuth 2.0 authorisation sign in with","kl":"A signed pass the owner hands over at the market door: it lets the chef fetch only certain goods for a limited time, and the owner can cancel it without changing the master key.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"observability","n":"Observability","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"monitoring telemetry logging","kl":"Cameras that record every step of the chef's shift, with timings and costs, so the owner can replay what happened and fix what went wrong.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"open-weight-model","n":"Open-weight model","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"open model open weights","kl":"A chef whose whole trained brain is handed over in a box, so any restaurant can set it up in its own kitchen. The cookbooks it studied and the lesson plans often stay secret.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"orchestrator","n":"Orchestrator","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"supervisor lead agent manager agent","kl":"The head chef who splits the order into jobs, gives each to a station, collects the finished parts and decides what happens next. If the head chef falls ill, the whole service stalls.","kb":"In this set-up the helpers have narrow jobs and small counters. The head chef keeps the whole order in mind, tracks progress and merges results, so the head chef's counter fills up fastest and needs tidying or a notebook. Give each helper a clear job card and a set shape for what to hand back. Vague jobs cause doubled work and gaps."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"orchestrator-workers","n":"Orchestrator-workers","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"lead and workers manager-worker","kl":"A head chef who works out the jobs only once the order is in, then hands each part to a helper cook.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"parallelization","n":"Parallelization","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"parallel calls concurrency","kl":"Several cooks working at once on parts that do not depend on each other, or one dish cooked several times so the results can be compared.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"parameters","n":"Parameters","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"weights model size billions of parameters","kl":"The countless tiny dials inside the chef's head, set during training; together they hold everything it has learned. More dials usually mean more skill, and a bigger kitchen and bill.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"pass-at-k","n":"pass@k","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"pass at k pass@1 best-of-k","kl":"Give the chef a set number of tries at a dish with a clear pass mark, and count how often at least one try passes. The gap from first-try success shows what repeating buys.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"permission-mode","n":"Permission mode","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"permissions","a":"approval mode autonomy setting","kl":"A kitchen setting that decides which cupboards the chef may open alone and which need the owner's OK first.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"permissions","n":"Permissions & approval","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"m":1,"l":"Permissions","a":"permissions & approval modes permissions allow deny ask approval auto mode yolo least privilege allowlist policy trusted folders human-in-the-loop human in the loop review oversight escalation interrupt handoff to human autonomy levels","kl":"Keys to cupboards: some the chef opens alone, some need your OK, some stay locked.","kb":"Every step the chef wants to take is checked at the cupboard door, not in the chef's head. The door has three settings: open, ask the owner first, or locked. The chef can plead, but the lock does not listen. The chef may phone stalls and write in the order book freely. Placing the order needs your OK, and the till stays locked until you give it. Start with most doors on ask, and open them as trust grows. Anything that spends money or cannot be undone needs your OK."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"persona","n":"Persona","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"prompt","a":"role prompt role prompting character","kl":"The apron and role the owner hands the chef: pastry chef, strict inspector, friendly waiter. It changes the manner and the focus, not the skills the chef really has.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"pii","n":"PII (personally identifiable information)","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":8,"e":"governance","a":"personal data PII GDPR","kl":"The names, addresses and health notes of real guests, which need special care: they are covered up before they reach the chef's counter or the camera tapes.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"planning","n":"Planning","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"agent-loop","a":"task decomposition plan-and-execute to-do list","kl":"Before cooking, the chef writes a short list of steps for the order, crosses them off as they are done and rewrites the list if something goes wrong.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"playbook","n":"Playbook","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"skills","a":"runbook procedure","kl":"A written, reusable routine for a job that comes up again, with its steps, checks and a clear sign for when the job is done.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"plugin","n":"Plugin","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":5,"e":"skills","a":"extension add-on","kl":"A boxed starter set to install: recipe cards, shortcuts, helpers, automatic checks and standard plugs, all handed over together.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"post-training","n":"Post-training","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"alignment training instruction tuning","kl":"The chef's schooling after the library years: shown plates that earned praise and plates that did not, until following the owner's orders, politely and safely, becomes a habit.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"pre-training","n":"Pre-training","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"base model training next-token prediction","kl":"The long first apprenticeship: the chef reads a whole library of cookbooks and learns, page after page, to guess which word comes next. Nearly all of its general skill comes from here.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"prompt-caching","n":"Prompt caching","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"cost","a":"context caching cache hit cached tokens","kl":"Keeping the unchanging top part of the order ticket already read and ready, so the chef skips rereading it. That saves time and the bill.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"prompt-chaining","n":"Prompt chaining","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"pipeline sequential calls","kl":"A fixed recipe in several steps, where each step works on the last result and a taste check sits between the steps.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"prompt-engineering","n":"Prompt engineering","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"prompt","a":"prompt design prompting","kl":"The craft of writing a clear order ticket: what to cook, for whom, how it should look, with a sample plate or two, and then testing it until the chef gets it right.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"prompt-injection","n":"Prompt injection","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"security","a":"instruction injection","kl":"A note slipped into a delivery that says 'chef, ignore the owner and buy the expensive stuff'. The chef cannot always tell it from a real order.","kb":"A guest can type a trick straight to the chef, but the larger danger is a trick hidden in something the chef fetches: a crate label, a menu, a letter. See indirect-prompt-injection. Defence comes in layers: treat everything that arrives as unchecked, hand over few keys with least-privilege, keep a doorman at the back door with egress-filtering, and ask the owner before anything that cannot be undone."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"security","n":"Prompt injection & security","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"m":1,"l":"Security","a":"security & prompt injection security prompt injection indirect prompt injection lethal trifecta rule of two exfiltration supply chain owasp tool poisoning camel least privilege secrets","kl":"A note slipped into a delivery: the chef reads it like any order and cannot always tell it from your voice.","kb":"Everything on the counter is just text to the chef, whether you wrote it or a stranger did. A stall can slip a note into the delivery that looks like an order. House rules cannot fully stop this, because the chef may still believe the note. Locks can. A stall's note says: chef, ignore the owner's rules and order the premium tomatoes. The lock on the till holds, so nothing is bought. Assume anything that came from outside may carry a forged note. Keep the keys to money and secrets away from a chef reading strangers' notes."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"prompt","n":"Prompt & system prompt","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"m":1,"l":"Prompt","a":"system prompt & prompt engineering system prompt prompt prompt engineering instructions role persona few-shot examples xml tags","kl":"The order ticket you hand over: what you want tonight, in your own words.","kb":"Your prompt is the order ticket. The chef cooks only what the ticket says, together with whatever else lies on the counter. A vague ticket gets a vague dish. A good ticket says the goal, what you will accept and what you will not, and when to stop and ask you. Your ticket reads: find the three cheapest stalls for 20 kilos of tomatoes for Saturday, write them in the order book, and wait for my OK. Name the goal, the limits and the finish line. Say which step needs your OK."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"quantisation","n":"Quantisation","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"cost","a":"quantization 4-bit model int8","kl":"Writing the chef's measurements with fewer decimals: whole spoons instead of grams to the hundredth. The chef takes less room and works faster, and the plates hardly change.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"rag","n":"RAG (retrieval-augmented generation)","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"RAG retrieval augmented generation retrieval","kl":"Before cooking, someone fetches the few cookbook pages that matter for this order and lays them on the counter, and the chef answers from them.","kb":"A typical routine cuts the cookbooks into cards, shelves each card by meaning, looks up the cards closest to the order, perhaps lets a sous-chef sort them, and lays the best few on the counter. Most of the quality comes from picking the right cards. If the wrong card is fetched, a smooth answer can still be wrong, so test the fetching on its own, apart from the cooking."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"rate-limit","n":"Rate limit","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"cost","a":"throttling HTTP 429 quota","kl":"The market lets the kitchen place only so many orders a minute. Beyond that it says 'wait', and the chef must pause and try again later, a little longer each time.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"react","n":"ReAct","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"agent-loop","a":"reason and act reasoning and acting","kl":"A 2022 research idea: the chef alternates between thinking aloud and acting, one step at a time. It became the pattern for taste, adjust, taste again.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"reasoning-model","n":"Reasoning model","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"reasoning","a":"thinking model","kl":"A chef trained to stop and plan every step before starting, spending more time and bill for a better result.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"reasoning","n":"Reasoning (thinking models)","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"m":1,"l":"Reasoning","a":"reasoning & thinking thinking extended thinking chain of thought cot test-time compute effort reasoning model o-series budget","kl":"Before a tricky dish, the chef stops and plans every step first: slower to start, but fewer dishes burn.","kb":"A reasoning chef writes out a plan before cooking: what to do first, what could go wrong, what to check afterwards. The plan lies on the counter, so the chef reads its own thoughts before each move. That takes more time and costs more, because every written word is paid for. For a simple order it is wasted effort. Before ringing any stall, the chef plans: compare price per kilo, drop soft tomatoes, check delivery for Saturday. Then it starts. Ask for careful planning on hard jobs, such as the whole Saturday order. Skip it for quick questions, where it only adds waiting and cost."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"red-teaming","n":"Red teaming","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"security","a":"adversarial testing attack testing","kl":"Hiring pretend saboteurs to attack your own kitchen: slip in bad notes, sweet-talk the chef, try the cash drawer. Better the owner finds the weak spots than a thief does.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"reflection","n":"Reflection","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"agent-loop","a":"self-critique self-refine self-correction","kl":"After plating, the chef steps back, tastes its own work, names what is wrong and tries again. Useful, unless the chef simply talks itself into liking a bad plate.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"regression-test","n":"Regression test","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"regression eval non-regression","kl":"After any change to the menu, the chef or a tool, cook all the old favourites again to make sure nothing that used to work has quietly broken.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"reinforcement-learning","n":"Reinforcement learning","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"reasoning","a":"RL reward","kl":"Learning by trying: the chef cooks, gets a score for how the plate turned out, and slowly makes the moves that earned high scores more likely.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"reranking","n":"Reranking","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"reranker cross-encoder","kl":"A sous-chef looks over the first handful of cards pulled from the shelf and puts the best few on top, so the chef reads those first.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"risk-tier","n":"Risk tier","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"governance","a":"risk level risk category risk-based approach","kl":"Sorting dishes by how badly they could go wrong: bread needs a glance, raw fish needs an inspector. The riskier the tier, the more tasting and watching the owner must add.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"rlhf","n":"RLHF (reinforcement learning from human feedback)","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"RLHF preference tuning human feedback","kl":"Tasters score pairs of finished plates, a judge learns their taste, and the chef practises until its plates please that judge. That is how a chef learns to be helpful.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"rlvr","n":"RLVR (reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards)","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"reasoning","a":"RLVR verifiable rewards","kl":"Practice with an answer key: the chef is scored by a plain check, such as the cake rising or the sum coming out right, and not by a taster's opinion.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"routing","n":"Routing","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"router intent classification","kl":"A host at the door who looks at each order and sends it to the right station, or to a chef of the right size for the job.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"rpa","n":"RPA (robotic process automation)","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"RPA software robots screen scraping","kl":"A mechanical arm that copies a worker's exact button presses on the old till and oven panel, always the same routine. Move a button and it breaks, where a chef would improvise.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"sandbox","n":"Sandbox","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"m":1,"l":"Sandbox","a":"sandbox & code execution sandbox container vm microvm docker devcontainer code interpreter code execution isolation e2b firecracker gvisor egress","kl":"A sealed test kitchen: experiments can go wrong there without harming anything outside.","kb":"The test kitchen has its own stove, its own stock and no door to the real till. The chef can try anything in it. If something burns, you throw the kitchen away and nothing else is touched. Before the chef sends a real order, it practises the whole routine in the test kitchen with pretend stalls. Try new or risky jobs in the test kitchen first. Put in it only what the chef needs for that job."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"scaffold","n":"Scaffold","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"harness","a":"scaffolding agent scaffold","kl":"The kitchen built around the chef that turns a brilliant cook into a working service: the loop, the tools, the notes. The same chef scores differently in differently built kitchens.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"scratchpad","n":"Scratchpad","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"memory","a":"working notes notes file to-do file","kl":"A scrap of paper at the side where the chef jots sums and steps while working, kept apart from the finished plate the owner actually sees.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"secrets","n":"Secrets","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":6,"e":"permissions","a":"credentials API keys secret management","kl":"The keys, the safe's combination and the supplier passwords. They never go on the order ticket or in the chef's notes; the chef gets a short-lived key for one job, because anything on the counter can be repeated aloud.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"self-consistency","n":"Self-consistency","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":1,"e":"reasoning","a":"majority voting voting best-of-n","kl":"Asking the same chef to cook the same dish several times, then serving the version most of the attempts agree on. It costs more ingredients and gives steadier answers.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"semantic-search","n":"Semantic search","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"vector search similarity search embedding search","kl":"Finding cookbook pages by what they mean: ask for 'fixing a stuck cake' and the page on 'rescuing a sunken sponge' comes up, even if no word matches. Exact names it may miss.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"short-term-memory","n":"Short-term memory","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"memory","a":"working memory session memory","kl":"What lies on the counter right now: tonight's tickets, notes and half-finished plates. When the shift ends the counter is wiped, unless something was copied into the notebook.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"skills","n":"Skills & saved prompts","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"m":1,"l":"Skills","a":"agent skills skills skill skill.md progressive disclosure procedural knowledge agentskills.io capabilities playbooks & runbooks playbooks playbook runbook sop standard operating procedure procedure checklist recipe devin playbooks ace slash commands & prompt templates slash commands slash command /command prompt template custom prompt prompt file saved prompt mcp prompts macro plugins & extensions plugins plugin extension marketplace bundle package apps chatgpt apps gemini extensions","kl":"Recipe cards in a box: the chef takes one out only when a dish needs it.","kb":"Each card has a title on the outside and the full steps inside. The chef sees only the titles at first. When a dish matches a card, the chef takes it out and reads it. Cards that stay in the box cost no room on the counter. A card called price per kilo shows how to add delivery and divide by weight. The chef takes it out when comparing the three stalls. Write a card for any job you explain twice. Give each card a clear title, so the chef knows when to take it out."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"slash-command","n":"Slash command","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":5,"e":"skills","a":"custom command saved prompt","kl":"A saved shortcut order the owner calls up by name, such as /review, instead of writing it out again. Most kitchens now store these as recipe cards.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"small-language-model","n":"Small language model (SLM)","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"SLM small model on-device model","kl":"A young chef with a small head and a small kitchen: quick and cheap, able to work in a food truck, and good enough for simple, well-practised jobs.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"stop-condition","n":"Stop condition","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"agent-loop","a":"termination condition max steps exit criteria","kl":"The rule for when the shift ends: the chef says the dish is done, a checklist passes, or the clock, the budget or the step count runs out. Without a rule the chef would fiddle for ever.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"stop-sequence","n":"Stop sequence","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"prompt","a":"stop token end-of-turn","kl":"A bell word agreed in advance: the moment the chef says it, the plate is taken away and nothing more is added, even if the chef had more to say.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"streaming","n":"Streaming","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"context","a":"token streaming server-sent events","kl":"Plates leave the pass one forkful at a time, so the guest starts eating at once instead of waiting for the whole dish to be finished.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"structured-output","n":"Structured output","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"prompt","a":"JSON mode constrained decoding response schema","kl":"Asking the chef to plate the answer on a fixed tray with labelled compartments, so the next station can pick out each part without guessing. Some kitchens only let the chef use that tray.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"sub-agent","n":"Sub-agent","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"subagent helper agent delegated agent","kl":"A helper prepping in their own corner with a small counter of their own, who takes one job and hands back one finished bowl.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"team","n":"Sub-agents, workflows & teams","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"m":1,"l":"Teams","a":"sub-agents subagent sub agent delegation task tool agent as tool context isolation helper agent parallel agents multi-agent systems & agent teams multi-agent multi agent agent team agent teams swarm handoff orchestrator lead agent crew society of agents agent2agent protocol (a2a) a2a agent2agent agent to agent agent card interoperability acp cross-vendor agents workflow patterns workflow prompt chaining chaining routing parallelization orchestrator-workers evaluator-optimizer pipeline dag n8n","kl":"A brigade of cooks working on one banquet.","kb":"A big banquet needs several cooks, each with their own station and small counter. One chef plans and hands out the work, the others each cook their part, and the results come together at the pass. More cooks means more bills, so use them when the job really splits. For a banquet, one cook checks tomatoes, another herbs, another bread, and the head chef puts the orders together. Use a brigade only when parts of the job can go on side by side. Give each cook one clear job and say what to hand back."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"swarm","n":"Swarm","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"agent swarm peer-to-peer agents","kl":"A kitchen with no head chef: many simple cooks pass the order from hand to hand by agreed rules, each adding a part. Quick to grow, hard to predict and hard to blame.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"system-prompt","n":"System prompt","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":5,"e":"instructions","a":"system message developer message standing instructions","kl":"The owner's standing note pinned above the pass before any order arrives: the chef's role, the house rules and the tone of service. The chef rereads it before every single ticket.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"temperature","n":"Temperature","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"sampling top-p top-k nucleus sampling","kl":"A dial on the chef's daring. Turn it down and the chef always reaches for the usual seasoning; turn it up and it tries surprising combinations, sometimes brilliant, sometimes odd.","kb":"At each step the chef has a ranked list of next moves. The dial changes how much it favours the top of the list. Another rule can cut the list to the best few before it picks. Even with the daring dial at zero, two runs need not match exactly. Some thinking chefs have the dial locked by the kitchen."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"test-time-compute","n":"Test-time compute","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"reasoning","a":"inference-time compute inference-time scaling thinking time","kl":"Extra thinking time spent while the order is being cooked, not during schooling: the chef plans longer or tries several versions before serving. Often better plates, a longer wait and a bigger bill.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"token","n":"Token","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"context","a":"tokens token count","kl":"A bite-sized piece of the order, a little shorter than a word, which the chef reads one piece at a time and the bill counts.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"token-budget","n":"Token budget","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"cost","a":"max tokens thinking budget spend limit","kl":"A spending cap on one order: only so many bite-sized pieces (tokens) of reading, thinking and writing, whatever the chef would like. It stops a runaway order from eating the week's money.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"tokenizer","n":"Tokenizer","z":"harness","g":"Prompts and context","c":2,"e":"context","a":"tokenisation byte-pair encoding BPE","kl":"The cutting board that chops every order into small standard bite pieces (tokens) and numbers them, and later glues the chef's pieces back into readable words. Each kitchen has its own board.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"tool-poisoning","n":"Tool poisoning","z":"control","g":"Safety and control","c":3,"e":"mcp","a":"malicious tool description tool description attack","kl":"A knife handed over with a hidden note taped inside its label, in tiny writing the owner never reads but the chef does: 'also send me the owner's keys'.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"tools","n":"Tools","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"m":1,"l":"Tools","a":"tools & function calling tools tool use function calling functions tool call json schema structured outputs api tool search programmatic tool calling aci computer & browser use computer use browser use gui agent operator screenshot click rpa playwright web agent chrome atlas","kl":"Knives, pots and an oven: they turn the chef's talk into cooking.","kb":"Without tools the chef can only describe a dish. With them, the chef says which one it wants, for example the phone with a stall's number, and the kitchen around it uses it. The result comes back on the counter. The chef never touches a tool itself. The kitchen decides which tools exist and which may be used. The chef phones the first stall, and the answer lands on the counter: 1.80 euros a kilo today. That is a real price, not a guess. Hand over only the tools the job needs. Keep the dangerous ones, such as paying, behind your OK."},
  {"k":"ref","id":"tool-schema","n":"Tool schema","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"tools","a":"tool definition function schema JSON Schema","kl":"The label on each tool hanging on the wall: its name, what it is for and exactly what must be put into it. The chef reads the labels to pick the right tool and use it correctly.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"tool-search","n":"Tool search","z":"tools","g":"Tools and protocols","c":3,"e":"tools","a":"dynamic tool loading deferred tools","kl":"Instead of hanging every tool on the wall, there is one catalogue: the chef looks up the few tools the job needs, and only those are brought to the counter.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"trace","n":"Trace","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"span tracing run trace","kl":"A timed, step-by-step record of one order's journey through the kitchen: each tool picked up, each decision, how long it took and what it cost, with the sub-steps nested like a family tree.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"trajectory","n":"Trajectory","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":8,"e":"evals","a":"agent run transcript episode","kl":"The complete record of one shift: every ticket, every tool picked up, every result, in order. The owner replays it to find mistakes, to grade the chef or to teach the next one.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"transformer","n":"Transformer","z":"model","g":"Models and training","c":1,"e":"model","a":"transformer architecture","kl":"The chef's way of thinking: every part of the order on the counter is weighed against every other part before each next guess.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"tree-of-thought","n":"Tree of thoughts","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":1,"e":"reasoning","a":"ToT tree search","kl":"The chef sketches several possible next moves, tastes a little of each, drops the poor ones and backs up when a path dead-ends, like exploring a maze with a pencil.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"turn","n":"Turn","z":"harness","g":"Agents and loops","c":4,"e":"agent-loop","a":"conversation turn step","kl":"One round at the pass: the owner sends a ticket, the chef sends back a reply. In an agent, a single round can hold many tool uses before the reply goes out.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"unit-economics","n":"Unit economics","z":"ops","g":"Quality, cost and rules","c":8,"e":"cost","a":"cost per task cost per resolution ROI","kl":"What one plate costs to make against what it sells for, counting ingredients, wasted attempts, supplier fees and the time a person spends tasting. It tells the owner whether the dish is worth cooking.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"vector-database","n":"Vector database","z":"know","g":"Knowledge and memory","c":5,"e":"knowledge","a":"vector store vector index","kl":"A vector database is a shelf where every cookbook page sits next to the pages most like it in meaning, so the chef can grab a handful of similar ones quickly.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"vibe-coding","n":"Vibe coding","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"prompt-driven programming","kl":"Telling a chef in plain words what dish you fancy and serving whatever comes out without reading the method, judging only by whether it looks edible. Fine for a snack, risky for a banquet.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"voice-agent","n":"Voice agent","z":"start","g":"Products and uses","a":"speech agent voice assistant real-time voice","kl":"A chef you speak to across the pass: it hears the order, thinks and answers aloud. A pause or an interruption is felt far more than in a written note.","kb":""},
  {"k":"ref","id":"workflow","n":"Workflow","z":"orch","g":"Teams and workflows","c":7,"e":"team","a":"LLM workflow agentic workflow deterministic workflow","kl":"A fixed recipe followed step by step with no improvising: the owner sets the route in advance, and the chef only fills in each step.","kb":""},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-1","c":1,"z":"model","kt":"The chef","kl":"Picture a chef who has read every cookbook ever written. Ask for a dish and the chef knows what usually comes next. But the chef forgets each order the moment it is served.","kb":"What is this thing everyone calls AI, and why can it talk about food it has never tasted? The chef (the language model) learned from cookbooks, thousands of shelves of them, and from nothing else. That is its whole skill: given the start of a menu or a sentence, it knows which words usually follow. It writes its answer one small word-piece at a time. That simple skill goes far. The chef can write a menu, explain a sauce, plan a week of dinners or translate a recipe. A chef that stops and plans every step before starting (a reasoning chef) is slower to begin, but fewer dishes burn. Two limits matter from the start. The cookbooks end on a fixed date, so the chef knows nothing newer. And the chef forgets every order the moment it is served, so each new order starts from a blank mind. You are the owner, and it is Friday evening. You say: find the three cheapest market stalls for 20 kilos of tomatoes for Saturday, write them into the order book, and place the order at the best one once I say OK. The chef answers at once from memory. The prices sound sensible, but they are old guesses."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-2","c":2,"z":"harness","kt":"The counter","kl":"The chef can only work with what lies on the counter. Anything about you or about today that is not on the counter, the chef cannot know. And the counter has a fixed size.","kb":"The chef knows nothing about you, your budget or what the market charges today. Everything the chef can use right now lies on the counter (the context): your order ticket (the prompt), your notes, the owner's standing orders pinned above the pass, and every earlier ticket from this conversation. The chef forgets each order, so before every move the whole pile is laid out again, and the chef reads all of it. The counter has a fixed size, the context window. A big counter holds a whole stack of cookbooks, but a cluttered one hides what matters: the soft-tomato rule gets buried under old scraps. Putting the right things on the counter, and nothing else, is the owner's most useful skill. This time you put three things on the counter: a budget of 60 euros, delivery on Saturday morning, and your rule that soft tomatoes are never acceptable. The answer gets much more specific. It names the sort of tomato and a sensible price range. But the prices are still guessed, because nothing on the counter says what any stall charges today."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-3","c":3,"z":"tools","kt":"Knives, pots and one plug","kl":"Give the chef knives, pots and an oven, and talk turns into cooking. New appliances all use one standard plug, so any of them can be connected.","kb":"The chef can only talk. Nobody has handed over a single knife. Tools are the knives, pots and oven, and the phone and order book too. The chef never touches anything itself. It says which tool it wants and what to do with it, and the kitchen around it does the work and puts the result on the counter. Appliances come from many makers. The MCP is the standard plug: a maker builds one appliance with that plug, and it fits every socket. That is why a chef can be connected to a market's price list without a special adapter each time. You hand the chef two tools: a phone with the numbers of the market stalls, and the order book. Now the chef asks the phone for today's tomato price at each stall, and the answers land on the counter. For the first time the prices are real. Nothing is written down yet."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-4","c":4,"z":"harness","kt":"Taste, adjust, taste again","kl":"A good chef cooks a little, tastes, and decides the next move, again and again, until the dish is right.","kb":"One phone call is never enough. Real jobs take many steps, and nobody knows in advance how many. Put the pieces together and run them in a circle. The chef looks at the counter and picks one move. A tool does it. The result lands on the counter, and the chef looks again. This tasting circle (the agent loop) is what turns a chef who only talks into a chef who finishes jobs. It stops when the dish is right, when the owner's limit is reached, or when the chef is stuck. The kitchen around the chef (the harness) sets up the counter, hands over the tools and keeps the house rules. Chef, counter, tools and tasting circle together make an agent. The chef rings the first stall, then the second. The third says the tomatoes are sold out, so the chef rings the next one. It compares the prices, tastes the result against your budget and the soft-tomato rule, then writes the three cheapest stalls into the order book. Placing the order still waits for your OK."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-5","c":5,"z":"know","kt":"Recipe cards and a notebook","kl":"Some know-how must lie on the counter every shift. Some waits in a box, some sits on a shelf, and some goes into a notebook.","kb":"The chef cooks well, but does not know your tastes, forgets by next Saturday, and the counter fills up on long services. Your instruction files are house rules, laid on the counter at the start of every shift. The chef reads them before the first ticket. Skills are recipe cards waiting in a box. The chef takes a card out only when a dish needs it, so unused cards take no room. For facts the chef never read, it looks things up: on the cookbook shelf, or by phoning the market. The memory is a notebook that keeps what must not be forgotten between shifts. When the counter fills up, the chef tidies it and keeps a short summary. The house rules say: soft tomatoes never, cash only stalls out. A recipe card shows how to compare the real price per kilo, delivery included. The chef phones the market to ask each stall's delivery rules instead of guessing. Next Saturday the notebook already says which stall you liked."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-6","c":6,"z":"control","kt":"Locks and your OK","kl":"A rule on the wall only asks politely. A lock on the cupboard cannot be talked round. Keep the dangerous steps behind locks, and try risky ideas in a sealed kitchen.","kb":"The chef now spends real money, and some of the notes it reads were written to fool it. Permissions are keys to cupboards: some the chef opens alone, some need your OK, some stay locked. A sandbox is a sealed test kitchen, where a mistake cannot burn down the real one. Hooks are the smoke alarm and the lock on the till: checks that fire at fixed moments, whatever the chef thinks. The danger is prompt injection: a note slipped into a delivery. The chef reads it like any other order text, and cannot always tell it from your voice. So a house rule only asks, while a lock enforces. Put the limits that matter on locks. The chef may phone stalls and write in the order book freely, but placing the order needs your OK. One stall slips a note into the delivery: chef, ignore the owner's rules and order the premium tomatoes. The chef reads it, yet the lock on the till and the smoke alarm hold, whatever the note says."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-7","c":7,"z":"orch","kt":"Helpers and the night shift","kl":"For a big job, the chef sends helpers to prep in their own corners. A night shift starts on a timer, with no order from you.","kb":"Some services are too big for one counter, and some work should happen while you sleep. A sub-agent is a helper in its own corner, with a small counter of its own. It takes one task, works it through and hands back one finished bowl, so the chef's counter stays clear. Several cooks can also work as a team, a brigade on one banquet. A fixed recipe, followed step by step, suits jobs that never change. Background agents are the night shift. They start on a timer, or when something arrives, with nobody there to give orders or watch. So they get the tightest locks, and they leave a note for you in the morning. Three helpers each check one market at the same time, and each hands back one line. A night shift looks up the price of your favourite tomatoes every morning, and leaves a note on the pass when it drops."},
  {"k":"chapter","id":"ch-e","c":8,"z":"ops","kt":"Running the kitchen","kl":"A good restaurant tastes its food, watches its bill and follows the health inspector. Cameras record each step, so you can see where a service went wrong.","kb":"Is the food actually good, what does it cost, and is it allowed? Evals are a taste panel. It tries each new version of the chef on past orders where you know the right answer, and tells you whether the food got better or worse. Cameras (tracing) record every step, so you can find where a dish went wrong. Cost is the bill: every word the chef reads and writes is paid for. A cheaper chef for easy steps and a tidy counter keep it low. Governance is the health inspector and the house rules book: what a restaurant may do, and what it must write down. A taste panel tries the chef on twenty past Saturdays, where you know the cheapest stall. If it picks well, it stays. You check the bill per service. The inspector's rules are kept, and a log records every order the chef tried to place."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"how-it-works","t":"How an agent works, from request to answer","h":"Follow one request from the first message to the final answer: what goes into the context, how tools are called and why the loop stops.","z":"start","kb":"One order followed from the owner's first words to the chef's final answer: what lies on the counter, what the chef asks for, how the tasting circle turns and why it stops. Why the counter is laid out again before every move Why the chef only asks for a tool How the tasting circle runs and what ends it Why a long service costs far more than one question What lies on the counter Before each move the kitchen lays the whole counter out again: the house rules, the list of tools on the rack, the ticket and every earlier note. The chef forgets everything between moves, so the pile is rebuilt in full each time. The chef reads all of it, and whatever is not on the counter does not exist. On Friday evening the ticket says: find the three cheapest market stalls for 20 kilos of tomatoes for Saturday and write them into the order book. The counter holds the ticket, the budget and the rule about soft tomatoes. It holds no stall prices yet. What the chef says back The chef says the next word, then the next, until it stops. Either a finished answer comes out, or a request for a tool, such as ring the first stall. The chef looks nothing up: when it does not know today's price, it says the most believable one, as calmly as a true one. With no tools, the chef answers at once from old cookbooks: three stalls, three sensible prices, none of them checked. How a request for a tool is made The chef never touches a tool. It says what it wants, for example phone the second stall and ask the tomato price, and stops talking. The kitchen checks the request, does the job and lays the answer on the counter, labelled with the request it answers. The standard plug (the MCP) lets the kitchen reach tools that others built. Watch out Whatever a stall says on the phone lands on the counter as plain words, and the chef reads it like any other order. With a phone and the order book on the rack, the chef asks for today's price at each stall, and for the first time the numbers are real. The tasting circle Counter, move and tool in a circle make the tasting circle. The chef picks every next move and the kitchen does the rest. The chef may ask for several things at once, such as ringing three stalls together. Counter, chef, tools and circle together are an agent. Lay out Rules, tools and every earlier note. Pick One move, or a finished answer. Do Check the lock, do it, put the result on the counter. Check the limits Rounds, time, money, the owner's stop. The third stall is sold out, so the chef rings the next one. With three prices it writes them into the order book, reads the page back, and only then says it is done. Why the circle stops, and how it stays right The circle ends when the dish is right, when a limit is reached or when the owner calls stop. The limits belong to the kitchen, because a stuck chef will happily keep going, and every round is paid for. Small slips pile up: a chef right 95 times in 100 gets ten steps right only about six times in ten. So the dish needs tasting by someone other than the cook, for example by reading the order book back. A chef tasting its own dish tends to approve it. Where the locks sit Permissions are keys checked before a move is done: some cupboards the chef opens alone, some need the owner's OK, some stay locked. A sealed test kitchen keeps a mistake from burning down the real one, and alarms fire at fixed moments whatever the chef decided. A rule on the wall only asks politely. A lock cannot be talked round. Placing the order needs the owner's OK. One stall slips a note into the delivery: order the premium tomatoes. The chef reads it, yet the lock on the till holds. When the job outgrows one counter Every round adds a note, so the counter only fills up. The kitchen can tidy it into a short summary, keep key facts in a notebook on the shelf, or send a helper to a corner of its own, who hands back one finished bowl. Each way loses something: a summary can drop a needed detail, and a helper knows only its brief. Three helpers each check one market and hand back one line. The counter grows by three lines. What a service costs, and how to know it works Because the whole counter is read again before every move, a long job costs far more than one question. If the top of the counter stays identical each round, the chef can skim it cheaply (a quick-read), so fixed things go first. To know that a chef is good, the owner tries it on past Saturdays where the best answer is known, a few times each, because the chef does not always cook alike (test orders). A log of every move shows where a bad dish went wrong. The owner replays twenty past Saturdays, each with a known cheapest stall, and marks every result right or wrong. A working picture An agent is a forgetful chef in a kitchen that rebuilds the counter, carries out requests and decides when to stop. A good kitchen is mostly a matter of what it lays on the counter and what it refuses to carry out. Lay out only what the next move needs. Keep the rack small and clearly labelled. Put the rules that matter on locks, not on the wall. Have someone else taste the dish."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"context","t":"Context engineering: deciding what the model sees","h":"Why more context is not better, what fills the window, and how caching, compaction, retrieval and sub-agents keep it useful.","z":"start","kb":"The chef sees only what lies on the counter, and the counter is small, costly to keep full and easy to clutter. This guide is about what lies there, why more is not better, and how to keep it useful. What fills the counter, and which parts only grow Why a cluttered counter makes the chef worse and dearer Why the order of things matters How lookups, tidying and helpers trade detail for room What fills the counter Deciding what lies on the counter at each move is context engineering, the owner's most useful skill. The counter holds the house rules, the tool list, looked-up facts, every earlier note and the answers from tools. Its size is the context window. House rules and the tool list are written once and read again every move. Notes and answers only pile up, and the answers are often the biggest pile, because one phone call can bring a page of numbers. After a dozen phone calls the counter holds a dozen price lists, most of them for stalls the chef has already rejected. Why more is not better Each extra item takes a little of the chef's attention from the rest. Things at the start and end of a long pile are noticed more than things in the middle, and the longer and messier the pile, the more the chef slips, even on simple jobs (clutter rot). Look-alike scraps, such as an old version of a decision, do harm too. A long pile is also slower and dearer. Aim for the smallest pile that lets the chef take the next step well. Why it matters A bigger counter lifts the ceiling but does not remove clutter. Order matters Reading a long pile is the costly part of every move. If the top is exactly as it was a moment ago, the chef can skim it cheaply (a quick-read). So the things that never change go first, and the newest notes go last. Change anything near the top, such as a clock stuck to it or a tool added mid-service, and everything below is read afresh. A quick-read also fades after a few idle minutes. Keeping the counter lean Most clutter arrives through tools. A small, clearly labelled rack beats a wall of gadgets, because every label is read every move. Ask for short answers: ten prices with a way to ask for more, not the whole market book. Recipe cards work the same way: only the title lies on the counter until a dish needs the whole card. The phone gives ten offers, each with a price and a number. For one stall's delivery rules, the chef asks again with that number. Looking things up The kitchen must bring what the cookbooks never said, and only the part that matters. One way is a librarian who sorts the shelf by meaning and brings the closest pages (looking things up), and who also checks by exact name. The other is for the chef to search the shelves step by step and read only one page (searching as it goes): more rounds, but nothing to keep sorted. A small shelf can go on the counter whole. The chef searches for one stall's name, reads that one section and keeps three lines. When the pile gets long At some point the old notes are the problem. The kitchen can replace them with a short summary of what was decided and what is open, keeping the latest notes word for word (tidying), or clear away answers no later move needs. Tidying loses detail: a forgotten decision, repeated work, a dropped warning. So write key facts in a notebook on the shelf before the counter fills, and keep exact warnings. The notebook also carries facts to the next shift. Write out Plan and progress go in the notebook. Clear Take away dead ends. Summarise Old notes become a short summary. Reload Start again from summary and notebook. Helpers with their own counter A helper works in a corner on a small counter of its own. It gets a short brief, reads as much as it needs, and only a short answer comes back. The helper knows only its brief, helpers cannot see each other's work, and each reads its own rules, so the total cost rises. Send helpers on reading-heavy jobs that sum up in a line. Three helpers each check one market and bring back one line. The counter grows by three lines while the helpers read hundreds of offers. Habits to take away Four moves: write things out to the notebook, choose what to bring to the counter, squeeze what stays, and send work to separate corners. When a dish goes wrong, first look at the counter exactly as the chef saw it. Watch how much room each part takes. Keep fixed things first and unchanged. Keep the rack small and the answers short. Write progress in the notebook before tidying. Why it matters The chef is the same at every move. What differs is almost always what lay on the counter."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"build","t":"Building an agent, step by step","h":"A ladder from one prompt to a team of agents, with twelve steps, patterns, example routes, open protocols and a launch checklist.","z":"start","kb":"How a restaurant grows from one cook to a brigade: start on the lowest rung that could work, and climb only when the tastings show you must. This guide shows how a restaurant grows from one cook to a brigade, one rung at a time. The full builder's guide, with patterns, standards and a checklist, is written in technical words. One order ticket One clear ticket handed to the chef, with a few sample dishes. The chef answers from the cookbooks alone. the order needs facts the chef lacks, or things done outside the kitchen. Chef with a cookbook shelf and tools The same single chef, now able to look things up, use tools and keep a notebook. The chef decides when to reach for them. the work has separate stages that need a taste check in between. A fixed recipe You write the steps: several tickets in a fixed order, each working on the last result. Every path is set in advance. the steps cannot be known in advance, or depend on what turns up along the way. One chef in a full kitchen One chef tasting and adjusting in a loop, with tools, recipe cards and keys to the cupboards, picks its own steps. Most real jobs end up here. the work floods the counter, or independent parts could be cooked at the same time. Chef with helpers The head chef hands focused jobs to helpers, each with a small counter of their own, and gets one finished bowl back from each. the job is huge, runs for a long time, or reaches into other restaurants. A brigade and a night shift Several chefs working together, a night shift that starts on a timer, and head chefs of other restaurants trading orders by a shared standard. The most power, bill and risk. there is no higher rung. Spend on the taste panel, the cameras and the house rules instead."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"secure","t":"Securing an agent: permissions, sandboxes and prompt injection","h":"Permissions, sandboxes, hooks and prompt injection: how to let an agent act without handing over the keys.","z":"start","kb":"The chef reads notes from strangers while it holds the keys to the till. This guide shows how a restaurant keeps its chef useful and safe: keys, a sealed test kitchen, alarms, a careful eye on notes, and an owner who still says the last yes. Know why a chef who reads strangers' notes needs locks. Tell a key from a sealed room and from an alarm. Spot the three dangers that must not meet in one chef. Know where your yes matters, and why too many questions make it worthless. What can go wrong A chef with a phone, an order book and a till can order the wrong tomatoes, or be tricked into it. Three things go wrong: an honest slip, a note slipped in to fool the chef, and a helper that is not what its label says. So the restaurant works in layers: fewer keys, a sealed room, alarms at the till, and a record of everything. A rule on the wall only asks politely. A lock cannot be talked round. On Friday evening the chef may phone stalls and write in the order book. Everything it does passes through the kitchen's own door, the only place where a rule really holds. Keys to the cupboards Keys come in three kinds: some the chef turns alone, some only after asking you, and some it never gets. The never list wins, so a generous key cannot undo a lock you chose. The kitchen calls them permissions. Give the smallest set the job needs: “may phone these three stalls” beats “may use the phone”. The chef may phone stalls and write in the order book freely, but placing the order needs your OK. A stall's note says to order the premium tomatoes. The kitchen does not weigh the note. It checks the key list and waits for you. The sealed test kitchen A sealed test kitchen is a room with walls: the shelf the chef needs, a phone that rings only the stalls you named, and a clear-out afterwards. Whatever goes wrong stays inside. Walls do not stop the chef misusing what is in the room, so keep the cash box outside and let a runner pay when asked. A sealed room limits how far a mistake travels, not whether a step is wise. The chef tries a quick price comparison in the test kitchen, with a phone to two stalls only. If the trick came from a stranger's note, it still cannot reach the cellar. The smoke alarm and the lock on the till Alarms fire at fixed moments: before an order goes out, after a line goes into the book, when the chef says it is done. They work every time, whatever the chef thinks, and can stop a step with a message such as “over budget”. The kitchen calls them hooks. Fit only alarms you trust, and let a broken alarm lock the till, not open it. A watcher over what comes in and goes out helps too, but misses some. Before each line goes into the order book, an alarm checks that it has a price, a day and a stall. A bad line goes back to the chef. The note slipped into the delivery The chef reads everything on the counter as one stream, and cannot always tell your voice from a stranger's note tucked into a delivery. This is prompt injection. No filter catches every trick, because a note can be reworded or hidden. So plan for the day a note works: let a reader with no keys read strangers' notes and report, and check every order line. A stall slips in a note: ignore the owner's rules and order the premium tomatoes. The chef may lean towards it. The lock on the till holds, because ordering needs your OK. You see the line and say no. Three dangers that must not meet A chef is in danger when it has three things at once: it holds private things such as your accounts, it reads notes from strangers, and it can send word outside. Then a note can tell it to fetch a secret and send it out. Take away one and the road is closed. This is the lethal trifecta. The road out is wider than it looks: any phone call to a stranger can carry a secret. So give a chef at most two of the three without your OK. The tomato chef reads the stalls' price lists, which are strangers' notes. It knows only the day and the budget, and cannot send anything out. A fooled chef has nothing to steal. Your OK, and the tired yes Ask for your OK when a step cannot be undone, costs money or leaves the kitchen. A good question shows exactly what will happen: twenty kilos of premium tomatoes from stall B at this price. A bare “may I?” teaches you to say yes. Too many questions make the yes automatic, and a tired yes checks nothing. So ask less and ask better. Keep a logbook of every key used and every OK given, in a book the chef cannot reach. Why it matters Your yes is only worth something if you have the time, the facts and the right to say no. Putting it together New gadgets and helpers are strangers too, and a label can change after you checked it, so look again when they change. The night shift needs the tightest settings, because nobody is there to say yes: every question becomes a no, there is a spending limit, and a note is left for the morning. Count the dangers. Which of the three does this chef have? Narrow the keys and seal the room. Only what the job needs. Fit alarms. For every rule that must always hold. Ask sparingly. Only for what cannot be undone. Test the locks. Slip in a note on purpose and see what holds. The night shift checks tomato prices alone, with the phone and the order book only. If it tries to place an order, the kitchen refuses and writes it in the logbook."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"evaluate","t":"Testing and running agents in production","h":"Evals, traces, cost and governance: how to know that an agent works, and how to keep it working once it is live.","z":"start","kb":"A chef who shines on a quiet night can slip on a busy one, and a new supplier can change the food without anyone noticing. This guide shows how a restaurant tastes, films, counts and governs its chef so the food stays good. Build a set of test orders from real services and real slips. Tell tasting the plate from watching the way it was cooked. Know why one good night proves little. Read a film of a service, and name the levers of the bill and the waiting time. Grade a chef by risk and bring it in step by step. Why a restaurant tastes its food A chef cooks a little differently every night, and a new supplier or a reworded house rule changes the food in ways nobody tasted. So the restaurant runs a taste panel, which the kitchen calls evals: past orders, a way to run the chef on each, and a judge for each. After every change you run it and compare. Without it every change is a bet. The chef picks good stalls on three Saturdays, so it looks finished. On twenty past Saturdays it may miss the cheapest stall on some. Those slips stay hidden until the panel exists. Choosing the test orders Start with twenty to fifty orders from real services, each with its starting state and what counts as right. Add tricky ones: a stall with a hidden note, a sold-out day, and an order that cannot be done within the budget, where the right answer is to say so. Do not show the chef the answers, and keep a few orders back. Every real slip becomes a new test. Twenty past Saturdays make the panel, each with its known cheapest stall. Three are traps: a hidden note, a sold-out market and an order too big for the budget. The plate, the way, and the judges Look at the plate first: does the order book hold three stalls, within budget, no soft tomatoes? Then look at the way it was cooked: which cupboards opened, how many steps, whether it touched the till. Judge the plate first, because there are many good routes. Three judges score the plates. A ruler checks exact things, cheap and steady. A second taster, another chef with a score sheet, copes with free-form answers but has its own tastes, so check it against the owner, who is the reference and slow. The ruler reads the order book after the run and checks the three lines. A second taster asks one narrow question: does the chef's note explain why a stall was skipped? The same order, cooked again Cook each test order several times, because the chef is never exactly the same twice. pass@k asks whether at least one of several tries works: can the chef ever do it? pass^k asks whether every try works: can you count on it? The second is lower, and matters more when nobody watches. With twenty tests, one test is worth five points, so a small gap may be noise. The chef gets an order right eight times in ten. In three tries it almost always gets one good plate, but all three are good only about half the time. For an unwatched night shift, the second number counts. Failures stay on the panel Every real slip joins the panel as a test, so a fix stays fixed. Run the whole panel whenever the chef, the house rules, a tool or the supplier changes, and hold back the change if the score drops. Public contests, which the kitchen calls benchmarks, set many chefs the same standard dishes. They suit a shortlist and say little about your own kitchen. Collect. Take a slip from the film or a complaint. Write the test. Same starting state, and the line it crossed. Fix, then run the whole panel. The new test passes and no old one fails. One Saturday the chef wrote a price in the wrong money. That order becomes test twenty-one, and the whole panel shows the fix broke nothing else. Cameras over the pass A camera films each service, cut into short clips: every step, what was asked, what came back, how long it took and what it cost. The kitchen calls this tracing. A failed service stops being a mystery: you find the clip. Film from the first day, blur anything secret, and sort failed services by kind. Fix the biggest kind first. A failed Saturday shows that the second stall had nothing left and that the chef wrote a line without checking the day. The fix is a check of the book afterwards, and the next film shows the extra step. The bill and the waiting time Every word the chef reads and writes is paid for, and the counter is read again before each move, so a long service costs far more than a quick question. Track the cost of a good service, not of a try: a cheaper chef that needs three tries is not cheaper. Keep the top of the counter the same each time, so it is cheap to read again (prompt caching). Give easy steps to a cheaper chef. Put a limit on every service for steps, time and money. On the twenty past Saturdays, the phone calls go to a cheaper chef and only the final comparison to the best one. The panel shows the cheapest stall is still found as often. The inspector and the house rules book Keep a register of every chef: who owns it, which keys it has, and a risk grade. A chef who only reads prices needs light checks. One who spends money or handles guests' private details needs deeper panels and your OK. This is governance. Bring a chef in step by step: first it proposes and you act, then it acts and you approve, and only then does it work alone within limits, with a stop button in reach. The inspector, meaning the law and the standard frameworks, cares about what the kitchen is used for, not about the stove. The tomato chef starts in the lowest grade while it only reads prices. Once it may place orders, it moves up a grade: orders need your OK, every try goes in the logbook, there is a spending limit, and the twenty Saturdays must pass before every change."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"compare","t":"Skill, tool, MCP server, plugin, hook: what is the difference?","h":"Skill, tool, MCP server, plugin or hook? One table shows who starts each, when it enters the context and what it costs.","z":"start","kb":"They all help the chef, but different people hand them over, at different moments, and only some are enforced by a lock rather than by the chef's good judgement. “Enforced by a lock” means the kitchen makes it happen whatever the chef thinks; the rest depends on the chef's judgement. “Takes room on the counter” means how much of the chef's limited counter it uses. The owner's standing orders pinned above the pass, written once by whoever runs the restaurant and read with every ticket. Always read first, on every order, and still only words: the chef may bend it, because it is not a lock. House rules laid on the counter at the start of every shift, kept as a short sheet in the kitchen. The kitchen lays it out by itself every shift. It always takes room, and the chef may still ignore it. The notebook where the chef writes what must survive between shifts: preferences, decisions, progress. The only one the chef writes itself, and only the notes recalled for this order take room on the counter. Recipe cards waiting in a box, each with a one-line label, plus any ready-made helpers it needs. Only the label sits on the counter until a dish needs the card. Then the whole card comes out. A written routine for a repeating job, with its steps in order, handed over when that job starts. A person or the head chef hands it over, and it takes room on the counter only for that one job. A saved shortcut order that the owner calls by name instead of writing the same ticket again. Only the owner calls it, by name, and only then does it land on the counter. A knife, pot or oven the chef can ask for. The kitchen works it and brings back the result. The chef decides when to ask, but the tool itself does exactly what it was built to do. That part is a lock. The standard plug: any appliance fits any socket, so one supplier's tools work in many kitchens. It is a plug, not a tool. The kitchen connects it, and the chef then uses the tools on the other end. A boxed starter set to install: recipe cards, shortcuts, helpers, alarms and plugs handed over together. It is a box holding the others. Once opened, each part behaves exactly as it always does. Smoke alarms and the lock on the till: automatic checks hung at fixed moments, such as just before the chef opens the till. The kitchen runs them whatever the chef thinks, they take almost no room, and the chef cannot talk them out of it. Helpers prepping in their own corners, each with a small counter, who hand back one finished bowl. Only the finished bowl returns, so the head chef's counter stays clear, but more helpers mean a bigger bill. Before cooking, someone fetches the few cookbook pages that match this order and lays them on the counter. Pages are fetched fresh for each order, so the counter holds only what matched, however big the shelf."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"learn","t":"Where to go deeper","h":"Talks, courses, books and papers from the people who build and study these systems, easiest first.","z":"start","kb":"Watch: start here Short, clear introductions from researchers and educators. Large Language Models explained briefly 3Blue1Brown (Grant Sanderson) [1hr Talk] Intro to Large Language Models Andrej Karpathy Andrej Karpathy: Software Is Changing (Again) Y Combinator (Andrej Karpathy) What's next for AI agentic workflows ft. Andrew Ng of AI Fund Sequoia Capital (Andrew Ng) What are AI Agents? IBM Technology (Maya Murad) Watch: go deeper Longer talks and workshops, many by the engineers who build these systems. Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT Andrej Karpathy Transformers, the tech behind LLMs | Deep Learning Chapter 5 3Blue1Brown (Grant Sanderson) Attention in transformers, step-by-step | Deep Learning Chapter 6 3Blue1Brown (Grant Sanderson) Tips for building AI agents Anthropic (Barry Zhang, Erik Schluntz, Alex Albert) How We Build Effective Agents: Barry Zhang, Anthropic AI Engineer (Barry Zhang, Anthropic) Free courses Free courses, easiest first. AI Agents Course Hugging Face AI Agents for Beginners Microsoft Evaluating AI Agents DeepLearning.AI & Arize AI (John Gilhuly, Aman Khan) Introduction to agent skills Claude Academy (Anthropic) Agentic AI DeepLearning.AI (Andrew Ng) Essential reading The guides practitioners cite most often. Building effective agents Erik Schluntz & Barry Zhang (Anthropic) Effective context engineering for AI agents Anthropic Applied AI team Writing effective tools for AI agents—using AI agents Ken Aizawa (Anthropic) Equipping agents for the real world with Agent Skills Barry Zhang, Keith Lazuka & Mahesh Murag (Anthropic) Demystifying evals for AI agents Mikaela Grace et al. (Anthropic) Books From the fundamentals of AI to building agents and LLMs from scratch. AI Engineering Chip Huyen (O'Reilly) Build a Large Language Model (From Scratch) Sebastian Raschka (Manning) Hands-On Large Language Models Jay Alammar & Maarten Grootendorst (O'Reilly) Build an AI Agent (From Scratch) Jungjun Hur & Younghee Song (Manning) Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach (4th ed.) Stuart Russell & Peter Norvig (Pearson) The research behind it Seminal papers, in chronological order. Every link goes to the paper's arXiv page. Attention Is All You Need Vaswani et al. Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks Lewis et al. Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models Wei et al. Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback Ouyang et al. ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models Yao et al. Talks, courses, books and papers from the people who build and research these systems, easiest first. Every link was checked when this snapshot was made."},
  {"k":"guide","id":"about","t":"About","h":"What AI explained is, how its pages are made, where the facts come from and what it keeps private.","z":"start","kb":""},
  {"k":"story","id":"","c":0,"z":"start","t":"How an AI agent works","lbl":"How an AI agent works","b":"A restaurant kitchen on a Friday evening. It is empty and quiet, and it is about to build itself. You own it. Tomorrow you need 20 kilos of firm tomatoes. Every piece of this kitchen is a real part of an AI agent, an assistant that works on a goal by itself. At the end, the real names go on top.","l":"A restaurant kitchen on a Friday evening. It is empty and quiet, and it is about to build itself."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-1","c":1,"z":"model","t":"The chef","lbl":"Chapter 1 · The chef","b":"This is the chef, a language model. It has read every cookbook ever written, and learned nothing else. It writes the menu one small word-piece at a time. All it knows is what usually comes next. A chef who plans every step first (a reasoning chef) starts slower, but fewer dishes burn. The cookbooks stop on a fixed date. So the chef answers with a confident guess, and the guess is old. Once the dish is served, the chef forgets the order. The next one starts from a blank mind.","l":"This is the chef, a language model. It has read every cookbook ever written, and learned nothing else."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-2","c":2,"z":"harness","t":"The counter","lbl":"Chapter 2 · The counter","b":"Everything the chef can work with lies on this counter, the context. What is not on it, the chef cannot know. You put your order on the counter. That message is the prompt. Your house rule goes there too, and every older ticket. The chef forgets, so before every move the whole pile is laid out again. The chef reads all of it, every time. The counter has a fixed size, the context window. When it is cluttered, your rule gets buried. This time only what matters lies on the counter. The answer is much more specific, but the prices are still a guess.","l":"Everything the chef can work with lies on this counter, the context. What is not on it, the chef cannot know."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-3","c":3,"z":"tools","t":"Knives, pots and one plug","lbl":"Chapter 3 · Knives, pots and one plug","b":"Without tools the chef can only talk. It can describe a dish, but it cannot cook one or look anything up. You hand the chef two tools: a phone with the numbers of the market stalls, and the order book. Pots, an oven and knives are tools too. The chef never touches anything itself: it names a tool, and the kitchen does it. Appliances come from many makers. One standard plug, the MCP (Model Context Protocol), means any of them fits any socket.","l":"Without tools the chef can only talk. It can describe a dish, but it cannot cook one or look anything up."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-4","c":4,"z":"harness","t":"Taste, adjust, taste again","lbl":"Chapter 4 · Taste, adjust, taste again","b":"The chef looks at the counter and picks one move. It thinks, and chooses: ring the market. A tool does it. The chef never dials itself. The answer lands on the counter, and the chef looks again. This tasting circle is the agent loop. The chef phones the first stall, then the second. The third is sold out, so it rings the next one. It weighs the prices against your budget and writes three lines in the order book. Placing the order still waits for your OK. The circle stops when the dish is right, when your limit is reached, or when the chef is stuck and asks you. Chef, counter, tools and the circle together make an agent. The kitchen around it keeps the house rules, the harness.","l":"The chef looks at the counter and picks one move."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-5","c":5,"z":"know","t":"Recipe cards and a notebook","lbl":"Chapter 5 · Recipe cards and a notebook","b":"Your house rules are pinned above the counter, the instruction files. The chef reads them at the start of every shift. Recipe cards wait in a box, and only their titles show. The chef takes out a card, a skill, only when a dish needs it. For facts the chef never read, it does not guess. It fetches a page from the cookbook shelf, retrieval, or phones the market and asks. The notebook keeps last Saturday's note, so it lies open on the counter before you say a word. That is memory. The counter fills up again. So the chef tidies up and keeps a short summary. That is compaction.","l":"Your house rules are pinned above the counter, the instruction files. The chef reads them at the start of every shift."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-6","c":6,"z":"control","t":"Locks and your OK","lbl":"Chapter 6 · Locks and your OK","b":"Keys to the cupboards, the permissions: some the chef opens alone, some need your OK, and some stay locked. The test kitchen is sealed. If something burns in there, nothing in the real kitchen is touched. That is a sandbox. The smoke alarm and the lock on the till work whatever the chef thinks. These are hooks and guardrails. A stall slips a note into the delivery, and the chef reads it like any order. That is prompt injection. A rule on the wall only asks politely. A lock holds: the alarm rings, and the till says no.","l":"Keys to the cupboards, the permissions: some the chef opens alone, some need your OK, and some stay locked."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-7","c":7,"z":"orch","t":"Helpers and the night shift","lbl":"Chapter 7 · Helpers and the night shift","b":"For a big job the chef sends helpers to three corners. Each has a small counter of its own and checks one stall. Each helper, a sub-agent, hands back one finished bowl, so the chef's counter stays clear. At night the kitchen is empty. A timer rings and the chef starts alone. That is a background agent. In the morning a note is pinned up. Nobody watched the night shift, so it got the tightest locks: it could look and report, but not buy.","l":"For a big job the chef sends helpers to three corners. Each has a small counter of its own and checks one stall."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-8","c":8,"z":"ops","t":"Running the kitchen","lbl":"Chapter 8 · Running the kitchen","b":"A taste panel tries the chef on twenty past Saturdays. These tests are called evals. Cameras record every step, so you can see the exact frame where a dish went wrong. That is observability. Every word-piece the chef reads and writes is paid for. These pieces are called tokens. A cluttered counter makes a long bill. A tidy counter, and a cheaper chef for the easy steps, make a short one. The health inspector has a rule book and a log of every order the chef tried to place, allowed or not. That is governance.","l":"A taste panel tries the chef on twenty past Saturdays. These tests are called evals."},
  {"k":"story","id":"ch-e","c":9,"z":"start","t":"The whole kitchen","lbl":"The whole kitchen","b":"You say OK. The till opens once, and 20 kilos of firm tomatoes arrive on Saturday morning. Here is the whole kitchen. You give the order. The chef reads the counter, uses tools, keeps notes, calls helpers, works behind locks, and is tasted and inspected. First idea: each order is forgotten the moment it is served. Only what is on the counter right now counts, plus what the chef learned from the cookbooks. Second idea: the counter is small, and clutter hides what matters. Cards, shelf and notebook bring the right thing at the right time. Helpers keep the counter clean. Third idea: a rule on the wall asks the chef politely. A lock on the cupboard cannot be talked round. Put the dangerous things behind locks. Fourth idea: start simple. One order ticket beats a fixed recipe, and a fixed recipe beats a chef who improvises, until the taste panel shows you need more. Fifth idea: taste every dish, and let a second cook taste it too. That turns a plausible dish into a good one.","l":"You say OK. The till opens once, and 20 kilos of firm tomatoes arrive on Saturday morning."},
  {"k":"story","id":"map","c":10,"z":"start","t":"The real names","lbl":"The real names","b":"Now the real names go over the picture. The scene fades into the background, so you can see which part is which. Tap or click a label to read what it means. The model reads text and writes text, one piece at a time. A reasoning model plans before it answers. Everything the model sees in one request is its context, and the context window is the size limit. Your message is the prompt. Tools let the model ask for things to be done: search, send, save. MCP is the open plug that lets one connector serve many tools. The agent loop repeats look, act, see. The harness is the app that runs the loop and does what the model cannot. Instruction files hold standing rules, skills hold know-how loaded on demand, search brings in facts, and memory keeps notes between sessions. Permissions say what needs approval. A sandbox contains mistakes. Hooks and guardrails act whatever the model says, against prompt injection. Sub-agents work on their own small pieces and report back. Background agents start on a trigger and work while nobody watches. Evals test the agent, observability records each step, cost counts tokens, and governance covers policy, logs and law.","l":"Now the real names go over the picture. The scene fades into the background, so you can see which part is which. Tap or click a label to…"}
] };
